detection whose \code{animal_id} the animals table does not list by its
transmitter, with a message that counts them.

\strong{Rows with no animal or no transmitter.} A row needs both. Rows that lack
one are dropped with a warning that counts each cause apart and names an
example, and the attribute \code{source} keeps the counts
(\code{dropped_no_animal_id}, \code{dropped_no_transmitter}). In an OTN sheet a
blank \code{TAG_CODE_SPACE} leaves the transmitter blank though the animal has
an id. With \code{tag_specs},
such a row takes its transmitters from the tag specifications by
\code{tag_serial}. A GLATOS detection export can leave \code{animal_id} blank on
every row; \code{missing_id = "transmitter"} then names each animal by its
transmitter, as glatos does. When every row would be dropped,
\code{read_animals()} stops. It stops too when more than half the rows have no
\code{animal_id}, with an error that names the columns it read the id from,
or no transmitter after \code{tag_specs} has filled what it can, with an
error that names the blank code space. Exactly half is a warning.

\strong{Tags that send two codes.} A tag with a pinger and a sensor, or two
sensors, sends two transmitter codes, and tagging sheets often list one.
With \code{tag_specs}, every code the specifications list for an animal's
\code{tag_serial} gets its own row on that animal, with the tag columns of the
specifications. The second codes can carry a large share of an
animal's detections.

\strong{Tag columns from the specifications.} Sensor calibrations and
transmission delays are not in the tagging sheets. They are in the
manufacturer's tag specification sheet, which \code{\link[=read_tag_specs]{read_tag_specs()}} reads.
With \code{tag_specs}, every row whose transmitter the specifications list
takes their tag columns (\code{tag_model}, \code{tag_life_days}, \code{min_delay},
\code{max_delay}, \code{sensor_type}, \code{sensor_unit}, \code{sensor_slope} and
\code{sensor_intercept}) where its own value is missing; the codes the sheet
lists and the second codes added from the specifications then agree. A
value the sheet gives is kept; where it differs from the specifications,
a warning counts the cells by column and names one. Tag serials of the
sheet that the specifications do not list get a warning too, sensor tags
first: such a tag loses its second code and its calibration, so find its
vendor sheet. A code the sheet gives for a serial whose specifications
list other codes, such as A69-9002-13710 where the specifications list
